# SYNOPSIS

```text
service NAME
program NAME
[directive ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

A manifest tells launcher(8) how to run one service: what program runs, what the process is given, and what it serves.
The launcher reads every manifest in bootfs's `etc/manifests/`, checks them against each other, and starts the services in an order in which each comes after what it uses.

A manifest is directives, one a line, read as every directive file is: a name, then words split at spaces and tabs; `#` and what follows it are a comment, and blank lines are skipped.
`service` comes first.
Anything wrong stops the launch with the file and the line; nothing is skipped.

: `service`
  The service's name, one word, unique among the manifests.
: `program`
  What runs: bootfs's `bin/<name>`.
: `arg`
  Arguments for the program, in order; each `arg` line adds its words.
: `env`
  One variable, `NAME=value`, set in the process's `/env`.
: `export`
  The service serves a Node tree that others may use.
  `export per-client`: it serves each client a tree of its own instead (launch-clients(5)). Its own tree is the launcher's alone: for each service that `use`s it, the launcher opens one through its `clients` node, naming that service and giving its `grant` lines, and mounts it at that service's `/svc/<name>`, so the service knows who is asking. A `mount` of a per-client service is an error, and so is a client it refuses.
: `grant`
  `grant <service> <words>`: what this service asks of a per-client service it `use`s, one line each, given to that service as the words after its name (factotum's key patterns, say). Granting what isn't per-client, or what it doesn't `use`, is an error.
: `use`
  A service's tree, granted at `/svc/<name>`, reconnected when that service restarts.
  `use <service> as <name>` grants it under another name.
  `use env` grants `/svc/env`, the launcher's own, whose `new` makes an environment group.
: `mount`
  `mount <path> <service>[/<sub>] [before|after] [create]`: a service's tree, or a directory of it, at <path>, alone or in a union before or after what is there, made if `create` is given.
: `srv`
  The session's board, at `/srv`, to post to and find others by.
: `restart`
  `never` (the default), `on-failure` or `always`.
: `early`
  Started before every service not marked early.
: `notegroup`
  `notegroup <service>`: the service's job is made under that service's, which starts first, so a note posted to that service's note group (proc(4)'s `notepg`) reaches it and what it starts too.
  The console's shell is in kbdfs(4)'s, whose Delete interrupts it.
: `resource`
  One kind of hardware: `mmio`, `irq`, `ioport`, `smc` or `system`, a range of the launcher's own root resource of that kind.
: `programs`
  bootfs with a job, to start programs from.
: `jobs`
  The launcher's own job, which holds every service and what each starts, to see and act on them: proc(4)'s.
: `bootdata`
  The firmware's boot data: the RSDP, the framebuffer, the memory map.
: `debuglog`
  The debuglog's ring and its doorbell, to read.
: `libraries`
  Shared objects from bootfs's `lib/`, to load.
: `bootfs`
  The boot image itself, read only, for the service that serves it.
: `launcher`
  The launcher's own tree at `/svc/launcher`: `status`; `memory`, a line for each service, `<name> processes <n> private <p> shared <s> scaled <x>` in bytes over its processes and those it started, then the launcher's own; and `ctl`, which takes `kill <service>`.
: `clock`
  The UTC page, writable: the service sets the system's time.

Every process is given the UTC page to read, whether or not its manifest asks.
A launcher with no resource, bootfs or boot data to give refuses a manifest that asks for one.

# EXAMPLES

The plumber, which serves its tree, starts programs from `/bin`, and is restarted whenever it fails:

```text
service plumber
program plumber
export
programs
mount /bin bootfs/bin
restart on-failure
```

# FILES

: `etc/manifests/*.manifest`
  In bootfs, the manifests the launcher reads.

